The part of “immutable” worth keeping is that once a branch splits, a later change in one branch doesn’t go back and edit the copies already passed down the other. So the family tree itself gives you a way to recover pieces of the past. And every generation gets handed a history already in progress. Nobody starts at block zero.

That’s the Pantheon thread I missed. A character can carry a piece of history they’ve never heard told. Then the family tells them a story that explains it perfectly. The drama starts when they meet a cousin whose branch makes that explanation impossible.